o the hot spots conjecture\n\nRami Atar, Krzysztof Burdzy\n\nAnalysis of PDEs · Submitted 14 Oct 2026 · 21 references\n\nPith's verdict: A promising counterexample with a load-bearing gap in the local boundedness estimate near the boundary.\n\nHow this review was made: An AI referee (deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash) read the paper on October 15, 2026 and answered a fixed list of questions about it. T0 is the starting level every paper gets. The levels describe how deeply Pith has checked the paper, never the authors.\n\n  1. T0 AI review An AI read the paper and answered a fixed list of questions.\n  2. T1 Audit Automatic checks that the references and files the paper relies on exist and match.\n  3. T2 Compute The paper's code or calculations re-run with the same results.\n  4. T3 Formal statement The claims written out in a formal math language a computer can read.\n  5. T4 Proof Those claims proved, and the proof checked by computer.\n\narXiv ID2610.19908 (version 1)\n\nPith IDBPZD56C7, Pith's permanent record of this paper and its review\n\nCitation Krzysztof Burdzy, Rami Atar. \"A starlike 3-dimensional counterexample to the hot spots conjecture.\" arXiv:2610.19908 (2026). https://pith.science/paper/BPZD56C7\n\n## Why Pith says this\n\nEditor's note read the letter\n\nThe paper constructs a starlike Lipschitz domain in R^3 that, if correct, would disprove the hot spots conjecture for starlike domains—a significant step beyond previus counterexamples that required holes or non-starlikeness. The overall strategy (building a domain with fine angular structure, convergence to a weighted eigenvalue problem whose first eigenfunction is radial) is clever and well laid out. The authors handle many details cleanly: the geometry lemma, the extension operator, the homogenization convergence, and the radial eigenvalue estimate.\n\nThe soft spot is Lemma 4, the local boundedness estimate for solutions on Ω_δ with Neumann conditions. The lemma states that the constant C is uniform in δ and holds up to the boundary. \nThe rest of the paper is careful, and the idea is important. I suspect the gap can be fixed with a more detailed boundary argument (e.g., reflecting across the Lipschitz boundary or using the available uniform extension operator). But as written, it is not complete.\n\nThe paper deserves a serious referee. The main task is to fill the gap in Lemma4. If that can be done, the result is a solid advance.\n\nWhat would settle it\n\nFor the constructed domain Ω_δ with fixed R (chosen large enough, e.g., R=10) and sufficiently small δ, compute numerically the first positive Neumann eigenfunction and check whether its maximum occurs at the origin or on the boundary. If a rigorous numerical computation shows that the maximum is on the boundary for a specific δ, the construction's uniform convergence argument would be contradicted.\n\nReferee report\n\nSignificance\n\nThis is a significant result: it resolves the hot spots conjecture in three dimensions for starlike domains, showing that failure can occur even under strong geometric constraints. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ninv lve\na journal of mathematics\nEditorial Board\nK (https://msp.org/involve/2020/13-1/involve-v13-n1-p.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Cassinian metrics are shown to be roughly similar to each other [Ibragimov 2016, ... In other words, if the domain ... For example, the one-point scale-invariant Cassinian metric, ˜τ p, is\n[Ibragimov 2016], satisﬁes both the triangle inequality and Ptolemy’s inequality.\nAnother reason is the fact that Euclidean spaces support full groups of Möbius\ntransformations. For example, the one-point scale-invariant Cassinian metric, ˜τ p, is\ninvariant under Möbius transformations, see [Ibragimov 2016, Lemma 2.1], which\nwas used in the proof of [Ibragimov 2016, Lemma 2.2]. Because of these reasons,\nthe distance function ˜τ c,a considered in Section 4